From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 17:37:28 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: complete image atomics before the thread's later
 accesses

Metal issues a texture atomic whose result the shader does not use
without waiting for it, and the texture fence the driver places after
every image atomic does not order it before the same thread's later
accesses to the texel. A load that follows can return the value from
before the atomic, and a store that follows can land before it. This
is a defect in the MSL KosmicKrisp generates, which relies on that
texture fence; a plain Metal program without Mesa reproduces it.

KHR-GLES31.core.compute_shader.resource-image hit this through Zink in
about one session in a hundred: its kernel zeroes a texel, adds the
coordinate sum atomically, reads the texel back and copies it to a
second image, and the copy held zero for one 8x4 block - one SIMD
group - while the first image held the sum. The standalone kernel
loses the same blocks in roughly one dispatch in 10,000 to 100,000 on
an Apple7 GPU under macOS 27.0.1, more often on a busy GPU. A
device-scope fence over device and texture memory removes the loss;
the texture fence, a texture-only thread fence and texture barriers at
threadgroup or SIMD-group scope do not.

Atomics on one texel stay ordered among themselves and one fence
completes all of a thread's earlier atomics, so the translation emits
that fence as KK_WORKAROUND_20 only once an atomic may be outstanding,
before the next image or global access, barrier or termination and at
the end of the shader. A kernel of nothing but unused atomics runs
1.8% longer, where a fence after every atomic cost 75%.

kosmickrisp_image_atomic_order checks every texel of 64,000 dispatches
each of the read and the overwrite shape, and with
MESA_KK_DISABLE_WORKAROUNDS=20 its --expect-loss mode reproduces the
loss. kosmickrisp_msl_image_atomic_order checks where the fence goes
for straight-line code, a trailing atomic and atomics in a loop.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
